<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
  <title>{{ title }}</title>
  <style>
    :root { color-scheme: light dark; --paper:#fff; --ink:#172033; --muted:#667085; --line:#e4e7ec; --blue:#2563eb; --blue-soft:#eff6ff; --gold:#b45309; --gold-soft:#fffbeb; }
    * { box-sizing:border-box; }
    body { margin:0; background:#f5f7fb; color:var(--ink); font:16px/1.65 Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif; }
    main { width:min(1040px, calc(100% - 32px)); margin:32px auto 72px; }
    header, section { background:var(--paper); border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:18px; margin:0 0 20px; padding:clamp(24px, 4vw, 48px); box-shadow:0 12px 35px rgba(16,24,40,.06); }
    header { position:relative; overflow:hidden; }
    header::after { content:""; position:absolute; width:220px; height:220px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--blue-soft); right:-75px; top:-95px; border:28px solid #dbeafe; }
    .eyebrow { color:var(--blue); font-size:.78rem; font-weight:800; letter-spacing:.12em; text-transform:uppercase; }
    h1 { font-size:clamp(2.1rem, 6vw, 4.2rem); line-height:1.02; letter-spacing:-.05em; margin:.3rem 0 1rem; max-width:760px; }
    h2 { font-size:clamp(1.45rem, 3vw, 2rem); line-height:1.2; letter-spacing:-.025em; margin:0 0 1rem; }
    h3 { font-size:1.08rem; margin:1.8rem 0 .4rem; }
    p { max-width:78ch; }
    .lede { color:var(--muted); font-size:1.12rem; max-width:72ch; }
    .chips { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:9px; margin-top:22px; }
    .chip { background:var(--blue-soft); border:1px solid #bfdbfe; border-radius:999px; color:#1d4ed8; font-size:.82rem; font-weight:700; padding:6px 11px; }
    .summary { border-left:5px solid var(--blue); padding-left:20px; }
    .metrics { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(190px,1fr)); gap:14px; margin:24px 0; }
    .metric { border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:14px; padding:18px; background:#fcfcfd; }
    .metric span { color:var(--muted); display:block; font-size:.82rem; font-weight:700; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.045em; }
    .metric strong { display:block; font-size:1.65rem; margin-top:4px; }
    .figure { margin:22px 0 30px; }
    .figure img { display:block; width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:13px; background:white; }
    .caption { color:var(--muted); font-size:.88rem; margin:.55rem .2rem 0; }
    table { width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:20px 0; font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums; }
    th, td { border-bottom:1px solid var(--line); padding:11px 9px; text-align:right; }
    th:first-child, td:first-child { text-align:left; }
    th { color:var(--muted); font-size:.8rem; letter-spacing:.04em; text-transform:uppercase; }
    .callout { background:var(--gold-soft); border:1px solid #fde68a; border-left:5px solid var(--gold); border-radius:12px; padding:16px 18px; }
    code { background:#f2f4f7; border-radius:5px; padding:2px 5px; }
    a { color:var(--blue); }
    .hash { color:var(--muted); font:12px/1.5 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Consolas, monospace; overflow-wrap:anywhere; }
    footer { color:var(--muted); font-size:.86rem; padding:14px 4px; }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme:dark) { :root { --paper:#111827; --ink:#f8fafc; --muted:#cbd5e1; --line:#334155; --blue-soft:#172554; --gold-soft:#451a03; } body{background:#0b1120}.metric{background:#0f172a}.chip{color:#bfdbfe;border-color:#1e40af}code{background:#1e293b}.figure img{filter:brightness(.94)} }
    @media (max-width:640px) { main{width:min(100% - 18px,1040px);margin-top:10px} header,section{border-radius:13px;padding:22px} table{font-size:.84rem} }
    @media print { body{background:white} main{width:100%;margin:0} header,section{box-shadow:none;break-inside:avoid} }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <header>
    <div class="eyebrow">European power-market research</div>
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    <p class="lede">Point-in-time-safe archived weather forecasts, DE-LU hourly day-ahead prices, rolling-origin validation, and a cost-aware ranking proxy.</p>
    <div class="chips"><span class="chip">6,551 delivery intervals</span><span class="chip">2025 common-coverage window</span><span class="chip">45-day holdout</span><span class="chip">No paid credentials</span></div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Technical summary</h2>
    <div class="summary">
      <p><strong>The weather-aware nonlinear model reduced untouched-holdout MAE by {{ "%.1f"|format(improvement) }}% versus the 48-hour seasonal-naive baseline</strong> ({{ "%.2f"|format(hgb_mae) }} versus {{ "%.2f"|format(baseline_mae) }} EUR/MWh).</p>
      <p>The final 45 delivery days were held out from model selection. Within-day rank correlation reached {{ model_rows[0].rank }}, which is the relevant signal for the deliberately simple top-three versus bottom-three hourly ranking proxy.</p>
      <p><strong>The backtest is a research abstraction.</strong> It charges explicit per-leg costs, but does not model exchange access, collateral, balancing exposure, liquidity, or executable order prices. The historical spread value must not be read as tradable profit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="metrics">
      <div class="metric"><span>HGB holdout MAE</span><strong>{{ "%.2f"|format(hgb_mae) }}</strong><small>EUR/MWh</small></div>
      <div class="metric"><span>Baseline reduction</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(improvement) }}%</strong><small>versus 48-hour lag</small></div>
      <div class="metric"><span>Active holdout days</span><strong>{{ strategy.active_days|int }}</strong><small>all gated complete</small></div>
      <div class="metric"><span>Shuffled placebo</span><strong>€{{ "{:,.0f}".format(placebo.net_total_eur) }}</strong><small>net proxy at €0.50/leg</small></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The nonlinear model adds information beyond the declared baselines</h2>
    <p>Every model sees the same chronological folds and the same fixed information cutoff. Histogram gradient boosting improves both absolute price error and within-day ordering, while the regularised linear model provides a transparent multivariate comparator.</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>MAE</th><th>RMSE</th><th>Daily rank correlation</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for row in model_rows %}<tr><td>{{ row.model }}</td><td>{{ row.mae }}</td><td>{{ row.rmse }}</td><td>{{ row.rank }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
    <p>The selected anomaly day is shown rather than a hand-picked profitable episode. Agreement on the broad daily shape matters more than matching every price spike, and the residual gap is visible rather than hidden.</p>
    <div class="figure"><img src="{{ figures['forecast_vs_actual.png'] }}" alt="Actual and forecast hourly prices on the selected weather anomaly day"><p class="caption">Actual versus all three predeclared comparators. Source: derived Energy-Charts/SMARD prices and Open-Meteo previous-run forecasts.</p></div>
    <p>Error is not uniform across the clock. The hourly comparison identifies where the nonlinear model narrows the baseline gap and where market dynamics still overwhelm the compact feature set.</p>
    <div class="figure"><img src="{{ figures['error_by_hour.png'] }}" alt="Mean absolute forecast error by local delivery hour"><p class="caption">Mean absolute error by local delivery hour over the final 45-day holdout; lower is better.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Transaction costs do not reverse the historical spread proxy</h2>
    <p>The signal takes one-MWh long positions in the three highest predicted hours and matching shorts in the three lowest predicted hours. At €0.50/MWh per entered leg, the ledger records €{{ "{:,.0f}".format(strategy.net_total_eur) }} of net historical spread value across {{ strategy.active_days|int }} holdout days, compared with €{{ "{:,.0f}".format(placebo.net_total_eur) }} for a seeded within-day shuffled placebo.</p>
    <div class="figure"><img src="{{ figures['equity_drawdown.png'] }}" alt="Cumulative cost-adjusted strategy proxy and drawdown"><p class="caption">Historical research-proxy accounting only; one MWh per selected leg and no leverage.</p></div>
    <p>Four discrete cost cases keep the position ledger fixed, so the bar comparison isolates the cost assumption rather than re-optimising the signal.</p>
    <div class="figure"><img src="{{ figures['cost_sensitivity.png'] }}" alt="Net research proxy by transaction cost assumption"><p class="caption">Net proxy result for €0.00, €0.50, €1.00, and €2.00 per entered hourly leg.</p></div>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Interpretation boundary:</strong> a high Sharpe ratio or perfect daily hit rate here reflects a stylised within-day spread-ranking exercise, not demonstrated executable alpha. Settlement prices are outcomes, not guaranteed fill prices.</div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Extreme-weather case study: {{ case.day }}</h2>
    <p>This delivery day had the largest holdout mean absolute weather-forecast anomaly score, calculated before inspecting strategy P&amp;L. The fixed-lag regional forecasts averaged {{ "%.1f"|format(case.temperature) }}°C, {{ "%.1f"|format(case.wind) }} km/h at 100 m, and {{ "%.0f"|format(case.solar) }} W/m² shortwave radiation.</p>
    <p>The DE-LU price averaged €{{ "%.2f"|format(case.actual_mean) }}/MWh and peaked at €{{ "%.2f"|format(case.actual_max) }}/MWh. HGB MAE was {{ "%.2f"|format(case.hgb_mae) }}, versus {{ "%.2f"|format(case.naive_mae) }} for Seasonal naive; the unchanged ranking rule produced €{{ "%.2f"|format(case.net_proxy) }} of cost-adjusted proxy value.</p>
    <p><strong>Largest thesis risk:</strong> weather at four representative grid points is not a load-weighted system forecast, and unmodelled outages, cross-border flows, fuel prices, and bidding behaviour can dominate the weather signal.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Scope and metric definitions keep the comparison auditable</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Product:</strong> hourly DE-LU day-ahead auction price in EUR/MWh for 1 January–30 September 2025; the later 15-minute market regime is excluded.</li>
      <li><strong>Decision cutoff:</strong> 12:00 Europe/Berlin on D-1. Predictive weather fields use Open-Meteo <code>previous_day2</code>, approximately 48 hours before validity.</li>
      <li><strong>Weather geography:</strong> equal-weight observations for Berlin, Hamburg, Frankfurt, and Munich; this is explicitly not labelled a load-weighted national index.</li>
      <li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> expanding rolling-origin folds, with the final 45 delivery days held out once for publication.</li>
      <li><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://api.energy-charts.info/">Fraunhofer ISE Energy-Charts / SMARD</a> and <a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/previous-runs-api">Open-Meteo Previous Runs API</a>; {{ source_request_count }} bounded requests are hashed in the fixture manifest.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Model specification and validation design</h2>
    <p>The comparators are a 48-hour price-lag Seasonal naive forecast, a median-imputed and standardised ridge regression, and a bounded histogram gradient-boosting regressor. Every transformer is fitted inside its training fold. The feature ledger rejects any row whose maximum source availability is later than the simulated cutoff.</p>
    <p>Reported forecast metrics are MAE, RMSE, median absolute error, and mean within-day Spearman rank correlation. Strategy accounting uses the HGB holdout predictions only, charges cost on every non-zero leg, and includes a shuffled-prediction placebo.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Limitations and robustness</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Historical association and predictive accuracy do not establish a causal weather-price relationship.</li>
      <li>The weather archive changes as numerical models evolve; the report therefore limits itself to a documented common-coverage window.</li>
      <li>The ranking proxy omits bids, fills, liquidity, fees beyond the declared per-leg cost, imbalance risk, collateral, and physical delivery.</li>
      <li>A 45-day holdout is informative but too short to establish persistence across years or market-design regimes.</li>
      <li>The 15-minute Single Day-Ahead Coupling regime beginning 1 October 2025 is a different product and is intentionally excluded.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Not financial advice.</strong> This is educational historical research and contains no live trading connection.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Recommended next steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add archived load, wind, and solar forecasts only after their original issue-time semantics can be proven.</li>
      <li>Evaluate prediction intervals and make the uncertainty kill switch active rather than optional.</li>
      <li>Model the post-1 October 2025 15-minute product as a separate regime with its own baselines.</li>
      <li>Replace the settlement-price proxy with an executable bid/fill model before making any economic claim.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Further questions</h2>
    <ul><li>How stable is the ranking improvement across seasons and scarcity regimes?</li><li>Which forecast horizon offers the best trade-off between cutoff safety and weather accuracy?</li><li>Does a calibrated no-trade threshold improve outcomes after realistic market-access costs?</li></ul>
    <p class="hash">Input SHA-256: {{ input_hash }}<br>Experiment manifest SHA-256: {{ manifest_hash }}</p>
  </section>
  <footer>Generated reproducibly by GridShock Research Lab {{ experiment_manifest.package_version }} from the committed attributed fixture.</footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
